<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Theoretical Justifications and Proofs</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section provides a detailed derivation of the theoretical insights presented in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Adam’s Second Moment as a Proxy for the Empirical Fisher ‣ 4 The OTA-Merging Framework ‣ Harnessing Optimization Dynamics for Curvature-Informed Model Merging" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, which establish Adam’s second-moment accumulator, <math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, as a principled proxy for the diagonal of the empirical Fisher Information Matrix (FIM).</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Proof of Equivalence between Hessian and Empirical FIM</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our first result connects the Hessian of the loss function to the Observed Empirical FIM at a fine-tuned model’s optimal parameters, <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. The Hessian for a loss <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{D}(\mathbf{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{D}(\mathbf{w})</annotation></semantics></math> over a dataset <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is given by:</p>
<table id="A1.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla^{2}\mathcal{L}_{D}(\mathbf{w})=\mathbb{E}_{(\mathbf{x},y)\sim D}\left[\underbrace{\mathbf{J}_{f}(\mathbf{w},\mathbf{x})^{\top}\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{z}}\ell(y,\mathbf{z})\mathbf{J}_{f}(\mathbf{w},\mathbf{x})}_{\text{Generalized Gauss-Newton (GGN) term}}\right]+\mathbb{E}_{(\mathbf{x},y)\sim D}\left[\underbrace{\sum_{j=1}^{C}\left(\nabla_{z^{j}}\ell(y,\mathbf{z})\right)\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{w}}f^{j}(\mathbf{w},\mathbf{x})}_{\text{Second-order term}}\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Generalized Gauss-Newton (GGN) term</mtext></munder><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Second-order term</mtext></munder><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla^{2}\mathcal{L}_{D}(\mathbf{w})=\mathbb{E}_{(\mathbf{x},y)\sim D}\left[\underbrace{\mathbf{J}_{f}(\mathbf{w},\mathbf{x})^{\top}\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{z}}\ell(y,\mathbf{z})\mathbf{J}_{f}(\mathbf{w},\mathbf{x})}_{\text{Generalized Gauss-Newton (GGN) term}}\right]+\mathbb{E}_{(\mathbf{x},y)\sim D}\left[\underbrace{\sum_{j=1}^{C}\left(\nabla_{z^{j}}\ell(y,\mathbf{z})\right)\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{w}}f^{j}(\mathbf{w},\mathbf{x})}_{\text{Second-order term}}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}=f(\mathbf{w},\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}=f(\mathbf{w},\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> are the model’s logits, <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> is the per-sample loss (e.g., negative log-likelihood), and <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{J}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{J}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Jacobian of the network function <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the parameters <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}</annotation></semantics></math>. To simplify this expression, we rely on two standard assumptions.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 1</span></span><span id="Thmassumption1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Late NTK Locality)<span id="Thmassumption1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Near an optimal set of parameters <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, the second-order term in Equation (<a href="#A1.E9" title="In A.1 Proof of Equivalence between Hessian and Empirical FIM ‣ Appendix A Theoretical Justifications and Proofs ‣ Harnessing Optimization Dynamics for Curvature-Informed Model Merging" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>), which depends on the curvature of the network function <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> itself, is negligible. This implies local linearity: <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\mathbf{x};\mathbf{w}_{*}+\bm{\delta})\approx f(\mathbf{x};\mathbf{w}_{*})+\mathbf{J}_{f}\bm{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>𝛅</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛅</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mathbf{x};\mathbf{w}_{*}+\bm{\delta})\approx f(\mathbf{x};\mathbf{w}_{*})+\mathbf{J}_{f}\bm{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> for small perturbations <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This assumption allows us to approximate the Hessian using only the GGN term:</p>
<table id="A1.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla^{2}\mathcal{L}_{D}(\mathbf{w}_{*})\approx\mathbb{E}_{(\mathbf{x},y)\sim D}\left[\mathbf{J}_{f}(\mathbf{w}_{*},\mathbf{x})^{\top}\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{z}}\ell(y,f(\mathbf{x};\mathbf{w}_{*}))\mathbf{J}_{f}(\mathbf{w}_{*},\mathbf{x})\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla^{2}\mathcal{L}_{D}(\mathbf{w}_{*})\approx\mathbb{E}_{(\mathbf{x},y)\sim D}\left[\mathbf{J}_{f}(\mathbf{w}_{*},\mathbf{x})^{\top}\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{z}}\ell(y,f(\mathbf{x};\mathbf{w}_{*}))\mathbf{J}_{f}(\mathbf{w}_{*},\mathbf{x})\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 2</span></span><span id="Thmassumption2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Perfect Calibration at Fine-Tuned Checkpoints)<span id="Thmassumption2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">At the optimal parameters <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, the model is perfectly calibrated, meaning its predictive distribution matches the true conditional data distribution for any given input <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(y|\mathbf{x};\mathbf{w}_{*})=d(y|\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(y|\mathbf{x};\mathbf{w}_{*})=d(y|\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">With these assumptions, we can now state and prove the main lemma.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1</span></span><span id="Thmlemma1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under Assumptions <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Late NTK Locality). ‣ A.1 Proof of Equivalence between Hessian and Empirical FIM ‣ Appendix A Theoretical Justifications and Proofs ‣ Harnessing Optimization Dynamics for Curvature-Informed Model Merging" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption 2 (Perfect Calibration at Fine-Tuned Checkpoints). ‣ A.1 Proof of Equivalence between Hessian and Empirical FIM ‣ Appendix A Theoretical Justifications and Proofs ‣ Harnessing Optimization Dynamics for Curvature-Informed Model Merging" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the Hessian of the loss at <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is approximately equal to the Observed Empirical FIM, <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{F}}(\mathbf{w}_{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐅</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{F}}(\mathbf{w}_{*})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">We begin by analyzing the inner term of the GGN in Equation (<a href="#A1.E10" title="In A.1 Proof of Equivalence between Hessian and Empirical FIM ‣ Appendix A Theoretical Justifications and Proofs ‣ Harnessing Optimization Dynamics for Curvature-Informed Model Merging" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>), which is the expectation of the Hessian of the negative log-likelihood <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=-\log p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=-\log p</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the true data distribution <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\sim d(y|\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\sim d(y|\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>. Using the identity for the Hessian of the log-likelihood, we have:</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{y\sim d(y|\mathbf{x})}[-\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{z}}\log p(y|\mathbf{z})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{y\sim d(y|\mathbf{x})}[-\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{z}}\log p(y|\mathbf{z})]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{y\sim d(y|\mathbf{x})}\left[-\frac{\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{z}}p(y|\mathbf{z})}{p(y|\mathbf{z})}+\frac{(\nabla_{\mathbf{z}}p(y|\mathbf{z}))(\nabla_{\mathbf{z}}p(y|\mathbf{z}))^{\top}}{p(y|\mathbf{z})^{2}}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{y\sim d(y|\mathbf{x})}\left[-\frac{\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{z}}p(y|\mathbf{z})}{p(y|\mathbf{z})}+\frac{(\nabla_{\mathbf{z}}p(y|\mathbf{z}))(\nabla_{\mathbf{z}}p(y|\mathbf{z}))^{\top}}{p(y|\mathbf{z})^{2}}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-\mathbb{E}_{y\sim p(y|\mathbf{z})}\left[\frac{\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{z}}p(y|\mathbf{z})}{p(y|\mathbf{z})}\right]+\mathbb{E}_{y\sim d(y|\mathbf{x})}\left[(\nabla_{\mathbf{z}}\log p(y|\mathbf{z}))(\nabla_{\mathbf{z}}\log p(y|\mathbf{z}))^{\top}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\mathbb{E}_{y\sim p(y|\mathbf{z})}\left[\frac{\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{z}}p(y|\mathbf{z})}{p(y|\mathbf{z})}\right]+\mathbb{E}_{y\sim d(y|\mathbf{x})}\left[(\nabla_{\mathbf{z}}\log p(y|\mathbf{z}))(\nabla_{\mathbf{z}}\log p(y|\mathbf{z}))^{\top}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">In the second line, we invoke Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption 2 (Perfect Calibration at Fine-Tuned Checkpoints). ‣ A.1 Proof of Equivalence between Hessian and Empirical FIM ‣ Appendix A Theoretical Justifications and Proofs ‣ Harnessing Optimization Dynamics for Curvature-Informed Model Merging" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (<math id="A1.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=p</annotation></semantics></math>) to switch the distribution of the expectation for the first term. This first term vanishes because the expectation of the score’s gradient is zero under standard regularity conditions:</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{y\sim p(y|\mathbf{z})}[\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{z}}\log p(y|\mathbf{z})]=\int\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{z}}p(y|\mathbf{z})dy=\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{z}}\int p(y|\mathbf{z})dy=\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{z}}(1)=\mathbf{0}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{y\sim p(y|\mathbf{z})}[\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{z}}\log p(y|\mathbf{z})]=\int\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{z}}p(y|\mathbf{z})dy=\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{z}}\int p(y|\mathbf{z})dy=\nabla^{2}_{\mathbf{z}}(1)=\mathbf{0}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">This leaves only the second term, which is the definition of the Fisher information matrix of the logits. Substituting this result back into Equation (<a href="#A1.E10" title="In A.1 Proof of Equivalence between Hessian and Empirical FIM ‣ Appendix A Theoretical Justifications and Proofs ‣ Harnessing Optimization Dynamics for Curvature-Informed Model Merging" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>), we arrive at the main result:</span></p>
<table id="A1.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\nabla^{2}\mathcal{L}_{D}(\mathbf{w}_{*})&amp;\approx\mathbb{E}_{(\mathbf{x},y)\sim D}\left[\mathbf{J}_{f}^{\top}\left(\mathbb{E}_{y\sim d(y|\mathbf{x})}[(\nabla_{\mathbf{z}}\log p)(\nabla_{\mathbf{z}}\log p)^{\top}]\right)\mathbf{J}_{f}\right]\\
&amp;=\mathbb{E}_{(\mathbf{x},y)\sim D}\left[\left(\mathbf{J}_{f}^{\top}\nabla_{\mathbf{z}}\log p\right)\left(\mathbf{J}_{f}^{\top}\nabla_{\mathbf{z}}\log p\right)^{\top}\right]\\
&amp;=\mathbb{E}_{(\mathbf{x},y)\sim D}\left[(\nabla_{\mathbf{w}}\log p(y|\mathbf{x};\mathbf{w}_{*}))(\nabla_{\mathbf{w}}\log p(y|\mathbf{x};\mathbf{w}_{*}))^{\top}\right]=\bar{\mathbf{F}}(\mathbf{w}_{*}).\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>f</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>f</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>f</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐅</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\nabla^{2}\mathcal{L}_{D}(\mathbf{w}_{*})&amp;\approx\mathbb{E}_{(\mathbf{x},y)\sim D}\left[\mathbf{J}_{f}^{\top}\left(\mathbb{E}_{y\sim d(y|\mathbf{x})}[(\nabla_{\mathbf{z}}\log p)(\nabla_{\mathbf{z}}\log p)^{\top}]\right)\mathbf{J}_{f}\right]\\
&amp;=\mathbb{E}_{(\mathbf{x},y)\sim D}\left[\left(\mathbf{J}_{f}^{\top}\nabla_{\mathbf{z}}\log p\right)\left(\mathbf{J}_{f}^{\top}\nabla_{\mathbf{z}}\log p\right)^{\top}\right]\\
&amp;=\mathbb{E}_{(\mathbf{x},y)\sim D}\left[(\nabla_{\mathbf{w}}\log p(y|\mathbf{x};\mathbf{w}_{*}))(\nabla_{\mathbf{w}}\log p(y|\mathbf{x};\mathbf{w}_{*}))^{\top}\right]=\bar{\mathbf{F}}(\mathbf{w}_{*}).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p4.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p4.4.1" class="ltx_text">Thus, the Hessian of the loss at an optimal point <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is approximately equal to the Observed Empirical FIM.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Proof of Relation between Mini-Batch Second Moment and FIM</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we show how the second moment of mini-batch gradients relates to the Empirical FIM defined above.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2</span></span><span id="Thmlemma2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\mathcal{L}_{B_{k}}(\mathbf{w})=\frac{1}{|B|}\sum_{(\mathbf{x},y)\in B_{k}}\nabla\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x},y}(\mathbf{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\mathcal{L}_{B_{k}}(\mathbf{w})=\frac{1}{|B|}\sum_{(\mathbf{x},y)\in B_{k}}\nabla\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x},y}(\mathbf{w})</annotation></semantics></math> be the gradient for a mini-batch <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|B|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|B|</annotation></semantics></math> sampled from the data distribution <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. Under Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption 2 (Perfect Calibration at Fine-Tuned Checkpoints). ‣ A.1 Proof of Equivalence between Hessian and Empirical FIM ‣ Appendix A Theoretical Justifications and Proofs ‣ Harnessing Optimization Dynamics for Curvature-Informed Model Merging" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the expectation of the outer product of this mini-batch gradient is a scaled version of the FIM:</span></p>
<table id="A1.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{B_{k}\sim D^{|B|}}\left[\nabla\mathcal{L}_{B_{k}}(\mathbf{w}_{*})\nabla\mathcal{L}_{B_{k}}(\mathbf{w}_{*})^{\top}\right]=\frac{1}{|B|}\bar{\mathbf{F}}(\mathbf{w}_{*})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐅</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{B_{k}\sim D^{|B|}}\left[\nabla\mathcal{L}_{B_{k}}(\mathbf{w}_{*})\nabla\mathcal{L}_{B_{k}}(\mathbf{w}_{*})^{\top}\right]=\frac{1}{|B|}\bar{\mathbf{F}}(\mathbf{w}_{*}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We decompose the expectation of the outer product:</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{B_{k}\sim D^{|B|}}\left[\nabla\mathcal{L}_{B_{k}}\nabla\mathcal{L}_{B_{k}}^{\top}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{B_{k}\sim D^{|B|}}\left[\nabla\mathcal{L}_{B_{k}}\nabla\mathcal{L}_{B_{k}}^{\top}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\left[\left(\frac{1}{|B|}\sum_{i=1}^{|B|}\nabla\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x}_{i},y_{i}}\right)\left(\frac{1}{|B|}\sum_{j=1}^{|B|}\nabla\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x}_{j},y_{j}}\right)^{\top}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\left[\left(\frac{1}{|B|}\sum_{i=1}^{|B|}\nabla\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x}_{i},y_{i}}\right)\left(\frac{1}{|B|}\sum_{j=1}^{|B|}\nabla\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x}_{j},y_{j}}\right)^{\top}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{|B|^{2}}\sum_{i,j}\mathbb{E}\left[\nabla\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x}_{i},y_{i}}\nabla\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x}_{j},y_{j}}^{\top}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{|B|^{2}}\sum_{i,j}\mathbb{E}\left[\nabla\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x}_{i},y_{i}}\nabla\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x}_{j},y_{j}}^{\top}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{|B|^{2}}\sum_{i=1}^{|B|}\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}_{i},y_{i}\sim D}[\nabla\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x}_{i},y_{i}}\nabla\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x}_{i},y_{i}}^{\top}]+\frac{1}{|B|^{2}}\sum_{i\neq j}\mathbb{E}\left[\nabla\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x}_{i},y_{i}}\nabla\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x}_{j},y_{j}}^{\top}\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{|B|^{2}}\sum_{i=1}^{|B|}\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}_{i},y_{i}\sim D}[\nabla\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x}_{i},y_{i}}\nabla\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x}_{i},y_{i}}^{\top}]+\frac{1}{|B|^{2}}\sum_{i\neq j}\mathbb{E}\left[\nabla\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x}_{i},y_{i}}\nabla\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x}_{j},y_{j}}^{\top}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since the samples in the mini-batch are i.i.d., the expectation of the cross-terms (<math id="A1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq j</annotation></semantics></math>) decouples:</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}\left[\nabla\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x}_{i},y_{i}}\nabla\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x}_{j},y_{j}}^{\top}\right]=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}_{i},y_{i}\sim D}[\nabla\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x}_{i},y_{i}}]\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}_{j},y_{j}\sim D}[\nabla\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x}_{j},y_{j}}]^{\top}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\left[\nabla\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x}_{i},y_{i}}\nabla\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x}_{j},y_{j}}^{\top}\right]=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}_{i},y_{i}\sim D}[\nabla\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x}_{i},y_{i}}]\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}_{j},y_{j}\sim D}[\nabla\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x}_{j},y_{j}}]^{\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">At the optimum <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, the expected gradient over the true data distribution is zero. This follows from Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption 2 (Perfect Calibration at Fine-Tuned Checkpoints). ‣ A.1 Proof of Equivalence between Hessian and Empirical FIM ‣ Appendix A Theoretical Justifications and Proofs ‣ Harnessing Optimization Dynamics for Curvature-Informed Model Merging" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and the fact that the expectation of the score function is zero:</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{(\mathbf{x},y)\sim D}\left[\nabla_{\mathbf{w}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x},y}(\mathbf{w}_{*})\right]=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}}\left[\mathbf{J}_{f}^{\top}\mathbb{E}_{y\sim p(y|\mathbf{x})}[\nabla_{\mathbf{z}}\log p(y|\mathbf{z})]\right]=\mathbf{0}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐰</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐉</mi><mi>f</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{(\mathbf{x},y)\sim D}\left[\nabla_{\mathbf{w}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x},y}(\mathbf{w}_{*})\right]=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}}\left[\mathbf{J}_{f}^{\top}\mathbb{E}_{y\sim p(y|\mathbf{x})}[\nabla_{\mathbf{z}}\log p(y|\mathbf{z})]\right]=\mathbf{0}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p2.4.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore, all the cross-terms (<math id="A1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq j</annotation></semantics></math>) in the decomposition vanish. We are left with only the diagonal terms of the sum:</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{B_{k}\sim D^{|B|}}\left[\nabla\mathcal{L}_{B_{k}}\nabla\mathcal{L}_{B_{k}}^{\top}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{B_{k}\sim D^{|B|}}\left[\nabla\mathcal{L}_{B_{k}}\nabla\mathcal{L}_{B_{k}}^{\top}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex10.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{|B|^{2}}\sum_{i=1}^{|B|}\mathbb{E}\left[\nabla\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x}_{i},y_{i}}\nabla\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x}_{i},y_{i}}^{\top}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{|B|^{2}}\sum_{i=1}^{|B|}\mathbb{E}\left[\nabla\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x}_{i},y_{i}}\nabla\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x}_{i},y_{i}}^{\top}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{|B|}{|B|^{2}}\mathbb{E}_{(\mathbf{x},y)\sim D}\left[\nabla\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x},y}(\mathbf{w}_{*})\nabla\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x},y}(\mathbf{w}_{*})^{\top}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{|B|}{|B|^{2}}\mathbb{E}_{(\mathbf{x},y)\sim D}\left[\nabla\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x},y}(\mathbf{w}_{*})\nabla\mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{x},y}(\mathbf{w}_{*})^{\top}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{|B|}\bar{\mathbf{F}}(\mathbf{w}_{*})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐅</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{|B|}\bar{\mathbf{F}}(\mathbf{w}_{*}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p2.5.1" class="ltx_text">This completes the proof, showing that the second moment of the mini-batch gradient is, on expectation, a scaled version of the full Empirical FIM.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Additional Information on Training SFT Models</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Gradient, and Loss Visualizations</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A2.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ B.1 Gradient, and Loss Visualizations ‣ Appendix B Additional Information on Training SFT Models ‣ Harnessing Optimization Dynamics for Curvature-Informed Model Merging" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> shows the training gradient norm trajectories <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\nabla\mathcal{L}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla\mathcal{L}\|</annotation></semantics></math> for each of our five specialist LLMs. Tasks like <span id="A2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">knowledge_recall</span> and <span id="A2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">coding</span> exhibit high gradient norms, whereas <span id="A2.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">math_reasoning</span> and <span id="A2.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">precise_if</span> remain much lower.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.11167v1/grad_norms.svg" id="A2.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:404/269;" width="404" height="269"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F8.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>: </span><span id="A2.F8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Gradient norm trajectories over global training steps for each fine-tuned SFT models</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, we visualize the optimization trajectories of each SFT model on distinct tasks as indicated in Figure <a href="#A2.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ B.1 Gradient, and Loss Visualizations ‣ Appendix B Additional Information on Training SFT Models ‣ Harnessing Optimization Dynamics for Curvature-Informed Model Merging" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F9" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.11167v1/appendix_specialist_losses_B1.svg" id="A2.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/257;" width="429" height="257"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F9.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 9</span>: </span><span id="A2.F9.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Training loss trajectories across global steps for each SFT model</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Experiments Details for Training SFT Checkpoints</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The models were trained from the <span id="A2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Meta-Llama-3.1--8B</span> base model using full-parameter Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) and AdamW optimizer. The second-moment statistics (<span id="A2.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">exp_avg_sq</span>) of the optimizer were checkpointed and later used as curvature proxies in OTA-Merging.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We leveraged full post-training stack provided by <span id="A2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLaMA-Factory</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al. [2024]</a></cite>. This unified infrastructure handled supervised fine-tuning (SFT), tokenizer alignment, and checkpoint conversion.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our configuration closely followed the hyperparameter recipe from the Tülu-3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lambert et al. [2024]</a></cite>, with slight task-specific adjustments. Fine-tuning was performed on two NVIDIA A100 GPUs (80GB) per task. We used gradient accumulation of 32 and a micro-batch size of 2 per device, yielding an effective global batch size of 128. All models were trained with a learning rate of <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math>, except for Precise Instruction Following (IF), which used <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math> to encourage faster convergence.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A2.T4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 4</span>: </span><span id="A2.T4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fine-tuning hyperparameters used for training specialist models.</span></figcaption>
<table id="A2.T4.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A2.T4.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T4.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="A2.T4.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hyperparameter</span></td>
<td id="A2.T4.5.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A2.T4.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Value</span></td>
<td id="A2.T4.5.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A2.T4.5.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Notes</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T4.5.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T4.5.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Base Model</td>
<td id="A2.T4.5.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">LLaMA 3.1–8B</td>
<td id="A2.T4.5.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_border_t"></td></tr>
